// simulated-user-research.dot
// ============================================================================
// Simulated User Research pipeline — attractor DOT graph.
//
// Encodes the proven manual process end-to-end:
//   0. RESET            (tool)      OPTIONAL project-supplied reset command,
//                                  runs before seed (see STAGE 0 below)
//   1. SEED             (tool)      resumable scratch-instance seeding
//   2. VISUAL CAPTURE   (tool)      single-shot subprocess, browser walkthrough,
//                                  2 viewports, notes (see MECHANISM CHANGE below)
//   3. DESIGN REVIEWS   (2x tool)  IA review + responsive review, PARALLEL,
//                                  single-shot subprocess + browser (see
//                                  MECHANISM CHANGE, 4th hardening cycle)
//   4. PERSONA SESSIONS (Nx tool)  sequential (single browser daemon), true
//                                  first-run onboarding + tasks per persona
//                                  (single-shot subprocess, see below)
//   5. SYNTHESIS        (box)      consolidated implementation-ready spec
//   6. HUMAN GATE       (hexagon)  approve / revise (loop, capped) / end
//
// THE .DOT IS THE LOGIC HOME. Do not reimplement this process elsewhere —
// change prompts/params/roster here and re-run.
//
// -- Design decisions worth reading before you edit -------------------------
//
// * MECHANISM CHANGE (3rd hardening cycle) — BROWSER NODES ARE NOW TOOL
//   NODES, NOT box (LLM/codergen) NODES: capture_screens and all three
//   persona nodes were originally box nodes with a full spawned child
//   agent session (loop-agent orchestrator + bash/filesystem/search),
//   driving `agent-browser` directly via bash inside that session. That
//   design is STILL correct in principle (zero-ceremony, single thread/
//   context) but has one fatal, UN-FIXABLE-AT-THE-PROMPT-LEVEL flaw:
//   loop-agent's coding-agent loop (amplifier-bundle-attractor/modules/
//   loop-agent/amplifier_module_loop_agent/agent_session.py.process_input)
//   treats ANY assistant reply with no tool call as natural session
//   completion — hardcoded, not gated by any SessionConfig field (checked
//   config.py: no "require tool call" / "auto-continue on text-only"
//   knob exists). Persona role-play makes models narrate a final
//   in-character observation with no paired tool call; three consecutive
//   hardening attempts (increasingly emphatic prompt-level warnings) died
//   on the exact same shape. The fix is STRUCTURAL, not prompt-level:
//   each of these four nodes is now shape=parallelogram (tool), shelling
//   out to scripts/run_browser_node.py, which runs the SAME task as a
//   completely separate, single-shot `amplifier run --mode single
//   --output-format json-trace` session under browser-node-agent.yaml (a
//   local overlay bundle in this same directory) and captures that
//   session's raw final response TEXT as the deliverable (json-trace,
//   not plain json, so the wrapper can also verify the browser genuinely
//   ran -- see the BROWSER-RAN PROOF header note below). A text-only final reply
//   is no longer a failure mode for these four nodes — it is the expected
//   way a single-shot session ends, and it's exactly what gets written to
//   the report file. See scripts/run_browser_node.py's module docstring
//   for the full investigation (why no loop-agent config knob exists) and
//   the "MECHANISM CHANGE" comment block on each converted node below.
//   The verify_* file-ground-truth quality gates (content-contract check
//   via scripts/validate_artifact.py + bounded retry + loud hard_stop)
//   downstream of each of these nodes keep the SAME shape (ok/retry/
//   hard_stop routing, bounded-retry counter, terminal hard_stop node)
//   as when this comment was first written -- only the judgment inside
//   the check changed (content contract, not byte floor; see the
//   HARDENING SPRINT header note above), never the retry/hard-stop
//   mechanics themselves.
//
// * MECHANISM CHANGE (4th hardening cycle) — REVIEW NODES CONVERTED TOO,
//   SYNTHESIS DELIBERATELY STILL UNCHANGED: review_ia and review_responsive
//   were ORIGINALLY left shape=box on the same justification synthesis
//   still carries below ("never exhibited the narration-without-a-tool-call
//   death shape in this pipeline's incident history"). That justification
//   EXPIRED: in round 4, review_responsive hit the narrate-and-die shape
//   THREE TIMES IN A ROW — status.json recorded outcome=success with
//   notes="Plain text response: Now I have all the information I need to
//   write the review. Let me compile the complete findings:" and NO
//   review-responsive.md was ever written, burning all 3 retries and
//   hard-stopping that branch (synthesis went on to correctly report it
//   under "Missing inputs" — the epistemic-honesty layer worked exactly as
//   designed, but the review itself was still lost for the round). See
//   verify-shots/research-round-4/.attractor-logs/r-20260724-205315/review_responsive/status.json.
//   Both review_ia and review_responsive are now shape=parallelogram (tool)
//   nodes, following the IDENTICAL structural fix as capture/persona below
//   — see their own node comments for the full mechanism-change writeup —
//   and, per the hardening brief, now ALSO drive a real browser (both
//   viewports) rather than only reading static capture-notes.md/screens, so
//   they get the same browser-ran proof manifest + zero-navigation refusal
//   as capture/persona, not a weakened variant of it.
//
//   synthesis remains shape=box (a real spawned loop-agent child session
//   via the pipeline engine's own session.spawn path) — it does not drive
//   a browser or role-play a persona, it reads static artifacts and writes
//   a structured document, and unlike review_responsive it has ZERO
//   narrate-and-die incidents in this pipeline's history. Its one
//   structurally-possible variant of the failure mode (a revision pass
//   that narrates instead of rewriting research-spec.md) is caught
//   separately by check_revision_progress's hash-staleness check (see
//   REVISION STALENESS below and that node's own comment) — converting it
//   would lose goal_gate/reasoning_effort/Missing-inputs prompt-level
//   nuance for no benefit today. If synthesis is EVER observed dying the
//   same way (text-only final reply, no report_outcome, mid-sentence) —
//   not just a stale-but-still-written revision — that is new evidence and
//   this decision should be revisited the same way the review nodes' was;
//   the wrapper script and overlay bundle already generalize for it (see
//   run_browser_node.py's module docstring, "--role synthesis WAS
//   CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT").
//
// * SEQUENTIAL PERSONAS (single browser daemon): the engine's concurrency
//   model is single-threaded at the top level — only ONE node executes at
//   a time UNLESS it is fanned out via shape=component (Section 3.8 of the
//   attractor spec). The persona nodes below are a plain linear chain
//   (never wrapped in a component/parallel node), so the engine itself
//   structurally guarantees they never overlap. No extra locking needed —
//   this IS the structural fix the design asked for.
//
// * SECOND SESSION-ISOLATION INCIDENT (6th hardening cycle) — SHARED-DAEMON
//   COLLISIONS COME FROM OTHER PROCESSES, NOT JUST OUR OWN CONCURRENCY:
//   round 7 produced a fabricated CRITICAL — "App navigates away to an
//   unrelated page on primary card click" — reporting the app under test
//   jumped to `http://localhost:8899/cortex-v1-mockup.html`. A bug-hunter
//   proved this was NOT a defect in the app: that URL/domain appear nowhere
//   in its source, and a clean isolated re-test showed
//   `window.location.href` unchanged with the card correctly opening its
//   detail rail. Actual cause: an UNRELATED CONCURRENT PROCESS on this
//   shared dev box issued bare `agent-browser open`/`click` commands (no
//   `--session` flag) against agent-browser's UNNAMED DEFAULT SESSION —
//   a single shared daemon per machine, per agent-browser's own docs — and
//   those actions landed in the same tab capture_screens/personaN's session
//   was driving. The mockup file genuinely exists elsewhere on this
//   machine; port 8899 is a long-running demo server from other,
//   unrelated work on that box.
//
//   The 4th hardening cycle already hardened this exact class of collision
//   for review_ia/review_responsive (see the CONCURRENT-BROWSER CAVEAT
//   comment on parallel_reviews below) by giving each review its own named
//   `--session review-{ia,responsive}` — but that fix only protects
//   concurrent reviews from COLLIDING WITH EACH OTHER. capture_screens and
//   the persona nodes were left on the unnamed default session because,
//   within THIS pipeline, they run strictly sequentially (see SEQUENTIAL
//   PERSONAS above) — which rules out self-collision but NOT a foreign
//   process on a shared machine. Worse, their setup/cleanup instructions
//   told them to run `agent-browser close --all`, which on a shared daemon
//   would also kill a browser some unrelated process on the box was using.
//
//   FIX (scripts/run_browser_node.py, 6th hardening cycle): capture and
//   every persona role now get their own dedicated named session too —
//   `--session capture` for capture_screens, `--session persona-<name>`
//   (e.g. `persona-marisol`) per persona, distinct enough to stay unique
//   when three personas run in the same round and human-legible in a log —
//   mirroring the pattern already established for reviews. Every
//   agent-browser command in those prompts now carries `--session`, and
//   `close --all` is banned from their prompt text (session-scoped close
//   only). See run_browser_node.py's module docstring, "NAMED SESSIONS FOR
//   CAPTURE/PERSONA WAS ADDED", and build_capture_instruction's /
//   build_persona_instruction's own docstrings, for the full mechanism.
//   The browser-ran proof counting regex (_AB_FLAG_RE) already tolerated
//   `--session <name>`-prefixed commands for ANY role since the 4th cycle's
//   review fix — no counting-logic change was needed, only the prompt text.
//
// * FILES ARE THE CONTRACT: every stage reads/writes real files under
//   $output_dir. Nothing depends on LLM context carrying prior conversation
//   forward (every LLM node below uses fidelity="truncate" — fresh session,
//   reads its inputs from disk). This is why truncation/fidelity mode
//   between nodes doesn't matter: the contract is the filesystem, not the
//   chat transcript.
//
// * RESUMABILITY: every stage has a file-existence guard node
//   (shape=parallelogram, "check_*") ahead of its work node, following the
//   proven graph-level self-skip pattern (see attractor's
//   examples/pipelines/12-graph-resume.dot). The standalone `attractor run`
//   CLI does NOT auto-resume from engine checkpoints — every invocation
//   restarts at Start. Resumability here is entirely the file-guard
//   pattern: delete an artifact to force that stage (and everything
//   downstream) to re-run; otherwise a re-run after a crash skips
//   everything already done, cheaply (a single `test -f` per stage).
//
// * REVISION LOOP CAP: the human gate's "Request Revision" path increments
//   a file-based counter ($output_dir/.revision-count) and hard-stops at 3
//   revisions (deterministic, inspectable — not an LLM-counted loop).
//
// * GRAPH-LEVEL retry_target/fallback_retry_target are deliberately LEFT
//   UNSET. If you set a graph-level retry_target for one stage's retry
//   needs, it becomes the fallback target for EVERY unrelated node's
//   unmatched-edge failure across the whole graph — a real footgun. Put
//   retry_target on the node that needs it (see `synthesis` below).
//
// * HARDENING SPRINT (3rd council round) -- ARTIFACT CONTRACTS REPLACE BYTE
//   FLOORS: every check_*/verify_* node below used to judge an artifact by
//   `test -f X && wc -c >= N` alone -- a byte count is a length check
//   wearing a completeness costume. Three councils independently found the
//   smoking gun: a showcase capture-notes.md passed its byte-floor gate at
//   1,800 bytes while documenting 4 of 11 screens and ending with the
//   literal line "## In Progress -- Additional Screens (to be completed)",
//   was re-grandfathered by the IDENTICAL resume guard on every later run
//   (check_ and verify_ shared the same floor, so a partial artifact could
//   never heal -- it entombed itself), and synthesis then certified
//   "Missing inputs: None." Every check_*/verify_* node below now calls
//   scripts/validate_artifact.py <type> <path> [--screens-dir D] [--run-id
//   R] [--findings-json F] instead -- ONE shared, content-aware contract per
//   artifact type, called from both the skip-guard AND the quality-gate.
//   That symmetry is the fix: a partial artifact now FAILS check_*'s
//   skip-guard too, so the stage is not skipped, it re-runs, and it heals
//   via the partial-draft-resume mechanism already built into
//   run_browser_node.py. See that script's module docstring for the full
//   per-type contract (required sections, cited-screenshot existence,
//   placeholder rejection, Run ID well-formedness, findings.json schema).
//
// * RESUME vs EXACT RUN ID CHECKING (4th council round -- the ONE
//   deliberate argument asymmetry between check_* and verify_*): a fresh
//   `attractor run` with a NEW run_id must still be able to resume past
//   a prior run's complete artifacts -- that is the entire point of the
//   check_*/verify_* skip-guard pattern. But the very first version of
//   the Run ID staleness check (previous bullet) applied ONE strict
//   comparison in BOTH check_* and verify_*, which broke exactly that:
//   a fresh run's check_capture rejected a complete, well-formed
//   capture-notes.md stamped with an EARLIER run's run_id as "stale",
//   forcing capture (and everything downstream) to re-run in full,
//   destroying both cheap resume and the read-spec/re-run/answer-the-gate
//   workflow. The fix is a mode flag on validate_artifact.py:
//     - check_* (skip-guards) call WITHOUT --require-exact (RESUME mode):
//       any WELL-FORMED r-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS stamp passes, from ANY run --
//       exactly what makes resuming past a prior run's artifacts possible.
//     - verify_* (fresh-production quality gates) call WITH
//       --require-exact (EXACT mode): the artifact's Run ID must equal
//       THIS run's run_id, or it fails as a stale write -- this is real,
//       load-bearing stale-write detection for a node that just claimed
//       to have (re)produced the artifact THIS run.
//   Both modes still reject an empty/placeholder/"unknown" Run ID and
//   every other content-contract failure -- only the cross-run identity
//   comparison is mode-dependent. See validate_artifact.py's module
//   docstring ("RESUME MODE vs EXACT MODE") for the full reasoning.
//
// * BROWSER-RAN PROOF: a dead target_url (or a model that narrates a
//   plausible-looking session without ever touching the browser) used to be
//   indistinguishable from a genuine walkthrough -- both produced a report
//   that read fine and passed a byte-floor check. capture_screens and
//   persona1-3 now invoke run_browser_node.py with `amplifier run
//   --output-format json-trace` (not plain json), giving the wrapper the
//   session's own tool-call trace -- real, ground-truth agent-browser
//   invocations and their real exit codes, not the model's self-report. Zero
//   real navigations or zero real screenshots -> the wrapper REFUSES to
//   write the report at all (loud stderr reason), so verify_<stage>'s
//   file-ground-truth check sees a missing/stale artifact and drives its
//   existing retry/hard-stop path. A `.session-manifest-<report-stem>.json`
//   sidecar is always written recording the raw counts for humans debugging
//   a hard-stop. See run_browser_node.py's module docstring, item 1.
//
// * RUN ID: every artifact this pipeline produces stamps "Run ID: $run_id"
//   in its header -- mechanically for the SIX run_browser_node.py
//   tool-node stages (capture, persona1-3, review_ia, review_responsive --
//   `_stamp_header` prepends it before writing, never left to model
//   compliance) and via explicit prompt instruction (the literal value
//   substitutes into the prompt before the model ever sees it, so the
//   model is asked to reproduce a value it was handed, not asked to
//   invent one) for the one remaining box node (synthesis). `run_id` is a
//   REQUIRED graph param with no default -- callers pass e.g.
//   `--param run_id=r-20260723-140501`.
//
// * REVISION STALENESS: gate_feedback's revision path now snapshots
//   research-spec.md's hash BEFORE re-running synthesis (snapshot_spec_hash)
//   and hard-stops loudly (check_revision_progress) if synthesis produced a
//   byte-identical spec -- catching the "failed/no-op revision silently
//   returns the stale spec" narrate-and-die class at the mechanism level
//   instead of trusting the model to notice its own non-change.
//
// * CLICK DISCIPLINE (5th hardening cycle) -- WHY THIS EXISTS, DO NOT
//   "SIMPLIFY" IT AWAY: a live bug-hunter investigation proved a
//   false-positive mechanism that had already polluted THREE CONSECUTIVE
//   ROUNDS (3, 4, 5) with fabricated CRITICAL findings -- "Rules Edit
//   button does nothing" (round 5 even marked it REPRODUCED across 2
//   sessions), "Hidden (N)/show never expands", and "watch toggle doesn't
//   update on first tap." All four were verified NOT REAL. Reproduced
//   byte-for-byte: clicking a control whose real getBoundingClientRect().y
//   sits BELOW the viewport (e.g. y=1250 in a 720px-tall viewport) inside
//   an internally-scrolling overlay whose scrollTop is still 0 --
//   agent-browser reports the click as done/success, but the click never
//   reaches the element and nothing happens; a DOM check
//   (offsetParent === null) then "confirms" the control is broken.
//   Scrolling the CORRECT scrollable ancestor (never "the page" -- these
//   overlays scroll internally) to its true max FIRST makes the identical
//   click work, first try, at every viewport width. BOTH false-positive
//   controls sat at the BOTTOM of a scrollable panel -- that is the
//   mechanism, not a coincidence. A second, corroborating artifact from
//   the same investigation: `agent-browser snapshot` (the accessibility
//   tree) has been observed NOT reflecting a genuinely-open overlay --
//   DOM class + computed style + a screenshot all agreed a panel was open
//   while snapshot still rendered the page underneath it -- so a single
//   "nothing changed" signal, from ANY one source, is not proof of
//   anything. The fix lives in scripts/run_browser_node.py's
//   _CLICK_DISCIPLINE_BLOCK, injected verbatim into ALL THREE of
//   build_capture_instruction, build_persona_instruction, and
//   build_review_instruction -- every role that drives the browser gets
//   the identical discipline: (1) scroll the element's real scrollable
//   ancestor to its true max and confirm the bounding box is in-viewport
//   BEFORE any click, (2) a click is never evidence by itself -- only an
//   explicit before/after state check (DOM class, computed style, visible
//   text, or screenshot) proves a click did or didn't do something, (3) a
//   "control does nothing" finding requires the bounding rect + scroll
//   position at click time, confirmation the element was in-viewport, AND
//   at least two independent no-change signals -- absent all three, the
//   finding must be downgraded or dropped, (4) the a11y snapshot alone is
//   never sufficient proof an overlay/modal is closed. This is
//   prompt-level discipline (this pipeline cannot mechanically verify a
//   session followed it) but it is the single highest-value lever against
//   re-introducing this exact false-positive class -- see
//   scripts/run_browser_node.py's own module docstring ("CLICK DISCIPLINE
//   WAS ADDED") for the full text and reasoning before touching it.
//
// * RETRY SUBSTRATE HARDENING: every retry-counter write below is now
//   `&&`-guarded (write to a .tmp file, `mv` into place, and only then
//   branch on the count) so that a FAILED counter write halts loudly
//   (hard_stop) rather than silently retrying forever with a counter that
//   never advances on disk. Every hard_stop_* node now also clears its own
//   counter file before halting, so a later legitimate re-run (after a
//   human fixes whatever caused the hard stop) gets a fresh retry budget
//   instead of hard-stopping again immediately on a stale count.
//
// ============================================================================

digraph SimulatedUserResearch {
    graph [
        goal="Run a simulated-user-research round against $target_url and produce an implementation-ready spec",
        label="Simulated User Research",
        rankdir=LR,
        default_fidelity="truncate",
        model_stylesheet="
            * { llm_model: claude-sonnet-4-6; llm_provider: anthropic; reasoning_effort: medium; }
            .synthesis { reasoning_effort: high; }
        "
        // NOTE (4th hardening cycle): a ".review { reasoning_effort: high; }"
        // rule used to live here, back when review_ia/review_responsive were
        // shape=box (LLM) nodes reading this graph-level model_stylesheet.
        // Both are now shape=parallelogram (tool) nodes that shell out to
        // scripts/run_browser_node.py's own single-shot session (fixed model
        // config lives in bundles/browser-node-agent.yaml, not here) -- a
        // ToolHandler node never consults llm_model/llm_provider/
        // reasoning_effort, so that rule became dead weight (harmless --
        // apply_stylesheet would still set the attr on the node, but nothing
        // reads it) and was removed rather than left to confuse a future
        // reader into thinking it still does something. See the "REVIEW +
        // SYNTHESIS" header note below for the full mechanism-change writeup.
        // NOTE: no graph-level retry_target/fallback_retry_target — see
        // header comment. Node-level retry_target is used instead where needed.
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Required params (pass via `attractor run --param key=value`, every run):
    //   run_id           -- this round's unique run identifier, e.g.
    //                       r-20260723-140501 (no default -- the CONTRACT
    //                       with the parallel lib/CLI build is that the
    //                       lib always passes --param run_id=r-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS).
    //                       Stamped as "Run ID: $run_id" in every artifact
    //                       header (mechanically for the SIX
    //                       run_browser_node.py tool-nodes -- capture,
    //                       persona1-3, review_ia, review_responsive -- via
    //                       run_browser_node.py's _stamp_header; via
    //                       prompt substitution for the one remaining box
    //                       node, synthesis) and validated by
    //                       scripts/validate_artifact.py on every
    //                       check_*/verify_* gate. verify_* gates pass
    //                       --require-exact: an artifact whose Run ID
    //                       doesn't match THIS run's is treated as a
    //                       stale write and fails. check_* skip-guards
    //                       omit --require-exact (resume mode): any
    //                       well-formed r-* stamp from a PRIOR run passes,
    //                       so a fresh run can still resume past already-
    //                       complete artifacts. See the header's "RESUME
    //                       vs EXACT RUN ID CHECKING" note above.
    //   target_url       -- base URL of the scratch instance to test
    //   api_key          -- API key/credential personas use to log in
    //   seed_command     -- full shell command (with its own flags) that
    //                       seeds/refreshes the scratch instance; must be
    //                       safely re-runnable (no-op on repeat is fine —
    //                       the guard below also skips it once seeded)
    //   personas_dir     -- absolute path to the folder of <name>.md briefs
    //   output_dir       -- absolute path for all artifacts this round
    //   app_source_hint  -- comma-separated file/dir path(s) the design
    //                       reviewers should read/cross-reference (the
    //                       app's source). Consumed by BOTH review tool
    //                       nodes (review_ia, review_responsive) via
    //                       run_browser_node.py's --app-source-hint flag.
    //   persona1/2/3     -- bare persona names (no .md); each must have a
    //                       matching $personas_dir/<name>.md brief file.
    //                       To change the roster size, copy a persona
    //                       check+work node pair (see PERSONA SESSIONS
    //                       below) and rename — DOT graphs are static, so
    //                       "N personas" means "author N persona nodes for
    //                       this roster."
    //   sur_repo_dir     -- absolute path to the amplifier-simulated-user-
    //                       research REPO ROOT (the directory containing
    //                       scripts/ and bundles/) -- NOT the
    //                       pipelines/ subdirectory this .dot file itself
    //                       lives in. Needed so the six run_browser_node.py
    //                       tool-node stages (capture, persona1-3,
    //                       review_ia, review_responsive) can find
    //                       scripts/run_browser_node.py. Not
    //                       auto-derived -- the engine has no "my own source
    //                       directory" var reliably available inside a
    //                       tool_command string (graph.source_dir is a
    //                       parser-level Python attribute, not a
    //                       $-substitutable context key). The L2/L4 layers
    //                       (amplifier_simulated_user_research) compute this
    //                       automatically and pass it as --param
    //                       sur_repo_dir=...; a direct `attractor run`
    //                       invocation must still pass it explicitly.
    //   browser_bundle   -- the bundle name or path run_browser_node.py's
    //                       --bundle flag should pass to
    //                       `amplifier run -B <browser_bundle>`. Register
    //                       bundles/browser-node-agent.yaml (in this repo)
    //                       once via `amplifier bundle add
    //                       file://$sur_repo_dir/bundles/browser-node-agent.yaml
    //                       --name simulated-user-research-browser-node`
    //                       then pass
    //                       browser_bundle=simulated-user-research-browser-node (or
    //                       pass the file://... path directly — both work,
    //                       proven live; the registered name is more
    //                       portable/readable in run invocations).
    //
    // Optional params (omit entirely for a project that doesn't need one):
    //   reset_command    -- OPTIONAL full shell command that resets the
    //                       target app's own state BEFORE seed_command
    //                       runs (4th hardening cycle -- generalizes
    //                       attention-firewall/server/scripts/reset_research.py;
    //                       see STAGE 0 below for the full contract and
    //                       rationale). Mirrors seed_command's own
    //                       param/plumbing shape exactly. When NOT passed
    //                       (the common case -- most projects have no
    //                       reset step), reset_state safely no-ops: see
    //                       reset_state's tool_command and the STAGE 0
    //                       header comment's "UNSET/EMPTY BEHAVIOR CANNOT
    //                       FAIL THE NODE" note for the exact mechanism
    //                       (do NOT reach for bash `${var:-default}`
    //                       shell-default syntax here -- it does not work
    //                       against this engine's substitution, see that
    //                       same note for why).
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------

    start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"]
    exit  [shape=Msquare,  label="Exit"]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 0 — RESET (OPTIONAL, deterministic, resumable; 4th hardening
    // cycle)
    // ==================================================================
    // Generalizes the lesson from attention-firewall/server/scripts/
    // reset_research.py: an un-reset research database silently corrupted
    // TWO CONSECUTIVE rounds (every push item had been dismissed days
    // earlier; rounds 3 and 4 both produced false "triage is broken"
    // findings from stale prior-session state, not a real bug). seed_command
    // alone cannot fix this class of problem -- seeding ADDS fixture data,
    // it does not UNDO the decision-derived state prior rounds left behind
    // (dismissed items, verdicts, transcripts). A project needs a separate,
    // project-specific way to wind its own state back to a representative
    // starting point BEFORE seeding runs.
    //
    // CONTRACT (this is the exact contract the lib/CLI side (RoundConfig)
    // must honor -- see amplifier_simulated_user_research/config.py):
    //   - param name: reset_command (exactly mirrors seed_command's own
    //     param name/plumbing -- same graph param mechanism, same
    //     to_dot_params() dict, same resolver.yaml `input: shortText` shape).
    //   - OPTIONAL: most projects have no reset step and MUST NOT be
    //     forced to supply one. RoundConfig.reset_command should default
    //     to None/"" and be OMITTED from to_dot_params() entirely when
    //     unset -- exactly how seed_command is currently ALWAYS included,
    //     but reset_command is included only when the project's YAML
    //     sets it.
    //   - UNSET/EMPTY BEHAVIOR CANNOT FAIL THE NODE: reset_state below
    //     never uses bash's `${var:-default}` shell-default syntax --
    //     THAT SYNTAX DOES NOT WORK HERE. The engine's substitute_context
    //     (substitution.py) does brace-content-as-literal-key lookup: a
    //     token written as `${reset_command:-true}` is looked up as the
    //     KEY "reset_command:-true" (colon-dash-and-all), which is never
    //     a real context key, so it is left COMPLETELY UNSUBSTITUTED and
    //     bash then sees the literal four characters `${reset_command:-true}`
    //     as a REAL, UNSET bash parameter reference -- which bash expands
    //     to empty ONLY under non-strict mode, but still leaves a
    //     confusing, easy-to-get-wrong dependency on bash's OWN parameter
    //     defaulting rather than the engine's. Proven live against the
    //     real substitute_context() before this landed -- do not
    //     reintroduce `:-` shell-default syntax into a tool_command
    //     expecting the ENGINE to resolve it; the engine only does plain
    //     key substitution (see substitution.py's own module docstring
    //     for the correct, portable pattern: `${optional_var:-fallback}`
    //     is a BASH-SIDE idiom for when bash itself receives the raw
    //     token unexpanded, not something the engine's substitution
    //     understands as a compound key). Instead, reset_state below
    //     captures the substituted value into a shell variable FIRST
    //     (`cmd="${reset_command}"`), then branches in bash on whether
    //     THAT variable is non-empty (`[ -n "$cmd" ]`) before ever
    //     `eval`-ing it -- this is safe whether reset_command was never
    //     passed at all (token left literal, but assigned as inert text
    //     into $cmd and never eval'd because the whole point is we branch
    //     BEFORE eval, not on the raw substituted text) or passed as an
    //     empty string. See the node's tool_command for the exact form.
    //   - WHEN SET: runs the caller's command verbatim, ONCE PER ROUND,
    //     BEFORE seed_command (never after -- a reset that ran after
    //     seeding would erase the very fixture data seed_command just
    //     wrote), and is itself resumable via the SAME
    //     file-existence-guard pattern as seed (`.reset-complete`
    //     marker) -- NOT unconditional on every invocation. This matters
    //     for crash-resume: the standalone `attractor run` CLI restarts
    //     every invocation at Start (see the header's RESUMABILITY
    //     note), so if reset_state had no skip-guard, resuming a round
    //     that already completed capture/personas would silently
    //     re-reset the target app's database out from under
    //     already-produced artifacts, corrupting the very consistency
    //     this stage exists to protect. check_reset below is what makes
    //     reset "once per round" rather than "once per invocation."
    //   - FAILURE MODE: if reset_command IS set and exits nonzero, this
    //     node's `&&` chain fails exactly like run_seed's does -- no
    //     retry_target reachable from it, so the pipeline halts loudly
    //     with the captured stderr tail. A broken reset must not silently
    //     let the round run against a still-corrupted dataset (same
    //     reasoning as run_seed's own comment below).
    check_reset [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Reset done?",
        tool_command="test -f \"${output_dir}/.reset-complete\" && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    reset_state [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Run Reset Command (optional)",
        tool_command="cmd=\"${reset_command}\"; if [ -n \"$cmd\" ]; then eval \"$cmd\" && mkdir -p \"${output_dir}\" && touch \"${output_dir}/.reset-complete\" && printf reset_ran || printf reset_failed; else mkdir -p \"${output_dir}\" && touch \"${output_dir}/.reset-complete\" && printf reset_skipped; fi"
    ]

    start -> check_reset
    check_reset -> reset_state [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_reset -> check_seed  [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]

    reset_state -> check_seed      [label="ran",     condition="context.tool.last_line=reset_ran"]
    reset_state -> check_seed      [label="no-op",   condition="context.tool.last_line=reset_skipped"]
    reset_state -> hard_stop_reset [label="failed",  condition="context.tool.last_line=reset_failed"]

    hard_stop_reset [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: reset_command failed",
        tool_command="printf 'FATAL: the project-supplied reset_command exited non-zero. This round cannot proceed against a dataset that may still carry prior-session state -- the exact class of bug this reset step exists to prevent (see attention-firewall/server/scripts/reset_research.py and rounds 3/4 false triage-is-broken findings). Inspect this node status.json/output.txt log for the actual stderr. No .reset-complete marker was written, so a later re-run (after a human fixes the underlying cause) retries reset_command rather than silently skipping it.\\\\n' 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 1 — SEED (deterministic, resumable)
    // ==================================================================

    check_seed [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Seed done?",
        tool_command="test -f \"${output_dir}/.seed-complete\" && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // Runs the caller-supplied seed command verbatim, then drops a marker
    // file. If seed_command fails (nonzero exit), the whole && chain fails,
    // ToolHandler returns FAIL, there is no matching edge for FAIL from this
    // node (its only outgoing edge is unconditional/success-routed), and no
    // retry_target is set anywhere reachable from it — the pipeline halts
    // loudly with the captured stderr tail. This is deliberate: a broken
    // seeder must not silently let the rest of the round run against a bad
    // dataset.
    run_seed [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Run Seed Command",
        tool_command="${seed_command} && mkdir -p \"${output_dir}\" && touch \"${output_dir}/.seed-complete\" && printf seeded"
    ]

    check_seed -> run_seed     [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_seed -> check_capture [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    run_seed -> check_capture

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 2 — VISUAL CAPTURE (LLM + browser via bash)
    // ==================================================================

    check_capture [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Capture done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" capture \"${output_dir}/capture-notes.md\" --screens-dir \"${output_dir}/screens\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MECHANISM CHANGE (see the graph header's "MECHANISM CHANGE" note for
    // full context): this was a shape=box (LLM/codergen) node whose prompt
    // instructed the model never to end on a text-only reply. That
    // instruction demonstrably decays across attempts inside loop-agent's
    // coding-agent loop: loop-agent (amplifier-bundle-attractor/modules/
    // loop-agent) treats ANY assistant reply with no tool call as natural
    // session completion -- hardcoded in agent_session.py.process_input,
    // NOT gated by any SessionConfig field (config.py has no "require
    // tool call" / "auto-continue on text-only" knob). Persona/capture
    // role-play reliably produces a final in-character or narrated
    // observation with no paired tool call, and 3/3 identical deaths were
    // observed in this pipeline's own run history despite an explicit,
    // emphatic prompt-level warning.
    //
    // STRUCTURAL FIX: this is now shape=parallelogram (tool node) that
    // shells out to scripts/run_browser_node.py, which runs the same task
    // as a completely separate SINGLE-SHOT `amplifier run --mode single
    // --output-format json-trace` session and captures that session's raw
    // final response TEXT as the deliverable -- written to capture-notes.md by
    // the wrapper script itself, not by the model remembering to call a
    // file-write tool as its last action. A text-only final reply is no
    // longer a failure mode for this stage; it is now the expected way a
    // single-shot session ends. See scripts/run_browser_node.py's module
    // docstring for the full investigation (why no loop-agent config knob
    // exists) and browser-node-agent.yaml for the bundle this shells out
    // to.
    //
    // The tool_command always exits 0 (note the trailing `|| true`):
    // exactly like the ORIGINAL box node, this node's own reported
    // outcome is NOT the quality gate -- verify_capture below is the sole
    // judge of whether capture-notes.md is real and sufficient, via a
    // content-contract check (scripts/validate_artifact.py, see the
    // HARDENING SPRINT header note) + bounded retry + loud hard_stop.
    // This node's job is only to run the
    // subprocess and get out of the way; retrying a bad/missing result is
    // still entirely verify_capture's job (its "retry" edge loops back to
    // this very node).
    capture_screens [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="capture browser",
        label="Visual Capture (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="4200",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role capture --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/capture-notes.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 4000 || true"
    ]

    check_capture -> capture_screens [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_capture -> parallel_reviews [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    capture_screens -> verify_capture

    // DEFECT 2 fix: the box node above can report outcome=success (turn
    // exhaustion, no report_outcome call -> engine's fail-open default,
    // see ROUTING-REFERENCE.md "Failure without report_outcome") without
    // ever having written its contracted artifact. Nothing upstream of
    // this point checks the FILE, only the reported status -- so the
    // pipeline previously marched on toward synthesis with a missing
    // input. This tool node re-checks the actual file on disk (ground
    // truth, not the agent's self-report) and routes on
    // context.tool.last_line:
    //   ok        -> proceed downstream
    //   retry     -> jump BACK to capture_screens itself (bounded; a
    //                fresh/resumed attempt that can see partial
    //                screens/ + a partial capture-notes.md on disk)
    //   hard_stop -> hard_stop_capture, which has no retry_target and no
    //                outgoing edges, so the engine halts the pipeline
    //                loudly (spec 3.7 step 4) instead of silently
    //                continuing with a missing artifact.
    // The retry counter is a file, not an LLM-counted loop (same
    // deterministic-counter pattern as check_revision_cap below); it is
    // cleared on success so a later independent run isn't penalized by a
    // stale count.
    verify_capture [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Capture artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" capture \"${output_dir}/capture-notes.md\" --screens-dir \"${output_dir}/screens\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_capture -> parallel_reviews  [label="ok",        condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_capture -> capture_screens   [label="retry",      condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_capture -> hard_stop_capture [label="hard stop",  condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    hard_stop_capture [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: capture-notes.md missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.capture-retry-count\"; printf 'FATAL: %s/capture-notes.md never satisfied its artifact contract after capture_screens ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). Visual capture cannot be considered complete -- downstream design reviews and synthesis have no capture artifact to read. Inspect %s/screens/ for partial output, .session-manifest-capture-notes.json for browser-ran proof counts, and the node status.json logs to see why capture_screens reported success without writing a contract-satisfying file (e.g. max_agent_turns exhaustion mid-walkthrough, or zero real browser navigations detected). The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run (after a human fixes the underlying cause) gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" \"${output_dir}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 3 — DESIGN REVIEWS (2 tool nodes, PARALLEL, single-shot
    // subprocess + browser -- see the "MECHANISM CHANGE (4th hardening
    // cycle)" comment on review_ia/review_responsive below for why these
    // are no longer LLM box nodes and no longer browser-free)
    // ==================================================================
    // shape=component fans out to ALL its outgoing edges concurrently.
    // Each branch is itself guarded (check_review_ia / check_review_responsive)
    // so a crash-resume skips a review that already completed, while the
    // OTHER review (if not yet done) still runs. Both branches converge at
    // the shared tripleoctagon fan-in node below (required — a component
    // node with no shared convergence node fails loud at validation/runtime).
    //
    // CONCURRENT-BROWSER CAVEAT (load-bearing, do not "simplify" away):
    // unlike the persona chain (STAGE 4), these two branches run IN
    // PARALLEL and BOTH now drive a real browser (agent-browser). Each
    // runs as a fully separate `amplifier run` subprocess, but
    // agent-browser's UNNAMED "default session" is a SINGLE SHARED DAEMON
    // PER MACHINE, not one per subprocess -- two concurrent subprocesses
    // both driving the default session would silently stomp on each
    // other's tabs/viewport/navigation state. This is NOT solved by
    // process isolation; it is solved by session isolation:
    // run_browser_node.py's build_review_instruction pins EVERY
    // agent-browser invocation in each review's prompt to
    // `--session review-ia` / `--session review-responsive` respectively
    // (agent-browser's own named-session feature gives each an isolated
    // cookies/tabs/viewport context) and explicitly forbids `close --all`
    // (which tears down every session on the machine, not just the
    // caller's own). See build_review_instruction's "NAMED SESSION, NOT
    // THE SHARED DEFAULT" docstring for the full mechanism and why this is
    // the one respect in which the review prompts deliberately do NOT
    // mirror capture/persona's `close --all` cleanup step.

    parallel_reviews [
        shape=component,
        label="Design Reviews (parallel)",
        join_policy="wait_all",
        error_policy="continue",
        max_parallel="2"
    ]

    check_review_ia [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="IA review done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" review \"${output_dir}/review-ia.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MECHANISM CHANGE (4th hardening cycle -- see the graph header's
    // "MECHANISM CHANGE" note and run_browser_node.py's module docstring,
    // "--role review WAS ADDED", for full context): this was a shape=box
    // node whose prompt begged the model never to end on a text-only
    // reply -- the SAME ask that failed for capture/persona in the 3rd
    // hardening cycle. The justification for leaving reviews as box nodes
    // ("they never exhibited the narration-without-a-tool-call death
    // shape") expired in round 4 when its sibling review_responsive hit
    // that exact shape three times in a row (see review_responsive's
    // comment block below for the incident evidence) -- review_ia is
    // converted alongside it rather than left as the sole surviving box
    // node exposed to an identical, already-proven failure mode.
    //
    // STRUCTURAL FIX: same as capture/persona -- now shape=parallelogram
    // (tool node) shelling out to scripts/run_browser_node.py --role
    // review --review-focus ia, a single-shot session whose raw final
    // response text IS the deliverable, written to review-ia.md by the
    // wrapper script itself. This review ALSO now drives the browser live
    // (both viewports) rather than only reading static
    // capture-notes.md/screens, so it gets the same browser-ran proof
    // manifest + zero-navigation refusal as capture/persona -- not a
    // weakened variant of it. verify_review_ia below remains the sole
    // quality gate; this node's tool_command always exits 0 (`|| true`)
    // so a subprocess-level failure routes through to verify_review_ia
    // (which correctly sees a missing/undersized file and drives the
    // retry/hard-stop path) rather than dead-ending on an unmatched FAIL
    // edge.
    review_ia [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="review browser",
        label="IA & Layout Review (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="1800",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role review --review-focus ia --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/review-ia.md\" --app-source-hint \"${app_source_hint}\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 1700 || true"
    ]

    // DEFECT 2 fix, review branch: same file-ground-truth verify pattern as
    // capture/persona (retry bounded at 2, then a loud stderr failure).
    // IMPORTANT ENGINE-SEMANTICS CAVEAT (documented here so it isn't
    // mistaken for a full pipeline halt): this node lives INSIDE the
    // parallel_reviews component branch, which uses
    // error_policy="continue" + join_policy="wait_all" by deliberate
    // design (see STAGE 3 header comment above) so one bad review never
    // blocks the other. _apply_join_policy's wait_all branch returns
    // SUCCESS/PARTIAL_SUCCESS for the aggregate parallel node even when a
    // branch's final outcome is FAIL -- PARTIAL_SUCCESS is then treated as
    // success for routing purposes (spec 4.5), so hitting hard_stop here
    // fails this ONE branch loudly (visible in logs/status.json/events)
    // but does NOT halt the overall pipeline the way the linear-chain hard
    // stops below do. That is intentional, not a gap: synthesis's prompt
    // (STAGE 5) now has an explicit "Missing inputs" instruction (DEFECT 2c)
    // that is the actual safety net for a review artifact lost this way --
    // it will be named as absent in the spec instead of silently omitted.
    verify_review_ia [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="IA review artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" review \"${output_dir}/review-ia.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_review_ia -> reviews_fan_in     [label="ok",       condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_review_ia -> review_ia          [label="retry",    condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_review_ia -> fail_review_ia     [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    // Terminal WITHIN this branch only (no outgoing edge) -- causes
    // run_subgraph to return this branch's outcome as FAIL, which the
    // wait_all/continue join policy folds into an overall
    // SUCCESS/PARTIAL_SUCCESS for parallel_reviews per the caveat above.
    fail_review_ia [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Branch FAIL: review-ia.md missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.review-ia-retry-count\"; printf 'FAIL (branch-local): %s/review-ia.md never satisfied its artifact contract after review_ia ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). This branch is marked failed; the OTHER review branch and the rest of the pipeline continue by design (error_policy=continue). synthesis will list this as a Missing input. The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    check_review_responsive [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Responsive review done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" review \"${output_dir}/review-responsive.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MECHANISM CHANGE (4th hardening cycle -- see the graph header's
    // "MECHANISM CHANGE" note and run_browser_node.py's module docstring,
    // "--role review WAS ADDED", for full context): this was a shape=box
    // node whose prompt begged the model never to end on a text-only
    // reply -- the SAME ask that failed for capture/persona in the 3rd
    // hardening cycle. The justification for leaving reviews as box nodes
    // ("they never exhibited the narration-without-a-tool-call death
    // shape") expired in round 4: review_responsive hit EXACTLY that
    // shape three times in a row (status.json: outcome=success,
    // notes="Plain text response: Now I have all the information I need
    // to write the review. Let me compile the complete findings:", no
    // review-responsive.md ever written), burning all 3 retries and
    // hard-stopping this branch. See
    // verify-shots/research-round-4/.attractor-logs/r-20260724-205315/review_responsive/status.json.
    //
    // STRUCTURAL FIX: same as capture/persona -- now shape=parallelogram
    // (tool node) shelling out to scripts/run_browser_node.py --role
    // review --review-focus responsive, a single-shot session whose raw
    // final response text IS the deliverable, written to
    // review-responsive.md by the wrapper script itself. This review ALSO
    // now drives the browser live (both viewports) rather than only
    // reading static capture-notes.md/screens, so it gets the same
    // browser-ran proof manifest + zero-navigation refusal as
    // capture/persona -- not a weakened variant of it. verify_review_responsive
    // below remains the sole quality gate; this node's tool_command always
    // exits 0 (`|| true`) so a subprocess-level failure routes through to
    // verify_review_responsive (which correctly sees a missing/undersized
    // file and drives the retry/hard-stop path) rather than dead-ending on
    // an unmatched FAIL edge.
    review_responsive [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="review browser",
        label="Responsive & Adaptive Review (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="1800",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role review --review-focus responsive --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/review-responsive.md\" --app-source-hint \"${app_source_hint}\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 1700 || true"
    ]

    // Same file-ground-truth verify pattern as verify_review_ia above.
    verify_review_responsive [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Responsive review artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" review \"${output_dir}/review-responsive.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_review_responsive -> reviews_fan_in       [label="ok",        condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_review_responsive -> review_responsive    [label="retry",     condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_review_responsive -> fail_review_responsive [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    fail_review_responsive [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Branch FAIL: review-responsive.md missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.review-responsive-retry-count\"; printf 'FAIL (branch-local): %s/review-responsive.md never satisfied its artifact contract after review_responsive ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). This branch is marked failed; the OTHER review branch and the rest of the pipeline continue by design (error_policy=continue). synthesis will list this as a Missing input. The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    parallel_reviews -> check_review_ia
    parallel_reviews -> check_review_responsive

    check_review_ia -> review_ia   [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_review_ia -> reviews_fan_in [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    review_ia -> verify_review_ia

    check_review_responsive -> review_responsive [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_review_responsive -> reviews_fan_in [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    review_responsive -> verify_review_responsive

    reviews_fan_in [shape=tripleoctagon, label="Reviews Collected"]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 4 — PERSONA SESSIONS (LLM + browser, STRICTLY SEQUENTIAL)
    // ==================================================================
    // A plain linear chain — NOT wrapped in shape=component — so the
    // engine's single-threaded top-level traversal is what guarantees
    // exactly one browser daemon runs at a time. To add/remove personas,
    // copy a check_personaN + personaN pair, renumber, and splice into
    // the chain (update the edge before and after it).

    reviews_fan_in -> check_persona1

    check_persona1 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 1 done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona1}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // MECHANISM CHANGE (same fix as capture_screens above): this was a
    // shape=box node whose prompt begged the model never to end on a
    // text-only reply. That is exactly the shape loop-agent's coding-agent
    // loop cannot be configured to reject (see capture_screens's comment
    // block and scripts/run_browser_node.py's module docstring for the
    // full investigation) -- and persona role-play is the WORST case for
    // it: a persona naturally narrates in character right up to its final
    // in-character observation, with no tool call paired to it. This is
    // now shape=parallelogram, shelling out to scripts/run_browser_node.py
    // for a single-shot session whose raw final response text IS the
    // friction-log deliverable, written to persona-$persona1.md by the
    // wrapper script itself. verify_persona1 below remains the sole
    // quality gate (content-contract check via scripts/validate_artifact.py
    // + bounded retry + loud hard_stop) -- this node's tool_command always
    // exits 0 (`|| true`) so a subprocess-level failure routes through to
    // verify_persona1 (which will correctly see a missing/undersized file
    // and drive the retry/hard-stop path) rather than dead-ending on an
    // unmatched FAIL edge.
    persona1 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="persona browser",
        label="Persona Session 1 (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="3600",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role persona --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona1}.md\" --persona-name \"${persona1}\" --personas-dir \"${personas_dir}\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 3500 || true"
    ]

    check_persona1 -> persona1 [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_persona1 -> check_persona2 [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    persona1 -> verify_persona1

    // DEFECT 2 fix, persona branch: file-ground-truth verify + bounded
    // self-retry + loud hard stop, same mechanics as verify_capture. This
    // sits in the STRICTLY SEQUENTIAL persona chain (not a parallel
    // branch), so hard_stop_persona1 halting here halts the WHOLE
    // pipeline (spec 3.7 step 4, no retry_target/fallback_retry_target
    // reachable from it) -- correct, since a missing persona artifact is
    // a genuine gap in the sequential chain's contract, unlike the
    // parallel review branches above where a sibling can still complete.
    verify_persona1 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 1 artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona1}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_persona1 -> check_persona2   [label="ok",       condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_persona1 -> persona1         [label="retry",    condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_persona1 -> hard_stop_persona1 [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    hard_stop_persona1 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: persona-1 artifact missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona1-retry-count\"; printf 'FATAL: %s/persona-%s.md never satisfied its artifact contract after persona1 ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). This is the sequential persona chain -- synthesis needs every persona report. Inspect node status.json logs, .session-manifest-persona-%s.json for browser-ran proof counts, to see why persona1 reported success without writing a contract-satisfying file (e.g. max_agent_turns exhaustion mid-session, or zero real browser navigations detected). The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" \"${persona1}\" \"${persona1}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    check_persona2 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 2 done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona2}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // MECHANISM CHANGE: same fix as persona1 above -- see persona1's
    // comment block and scripts/run_browser_node.py's module docstring
    // for the full investigation.
    persona2 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="persona browser",
        label="Persona Session 2 (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="3600",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role persona --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona2}.md\" --persona-name \"${persona2}\" --personas-dir \"${personas_dir}\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 3500 || true"
    ]

    check_persona2 -> persona2 [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_persona2 -> check_persona3 [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    persona2 -> verify_persona2

    verify_persona2 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 2 artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona2}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_persona2 -> check_persona3   [label="ok",       condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_persona2 -> persona2         [label="retry",    condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_persona2 -> hard_stop_persona2 [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    hard_stop_persona2 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: persona-2 artifact missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona2-retry-count\"; printf 'FATAL: %s/persona-%s.md never satisfied its artifact contract after persona2 ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). This is the sequential persona chain -- synthesis needs every persona report. Inspect node status.json logs, .session-manifest-persona-%s.json for browser-ran proof counts, to see why persona2 reported success without writing a contract-satisfying file (e.g. max_agent_turns exhaustion mid-session, or zero real browser navigations detected). The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" \"${persona2}\" \"${persona2}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    check_persona3 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 3 done?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona3}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || printf todo"
    ]

    // MECHANISM CHANGE: same fix as persona1/persona2 above -- see
    // persona1's comment block and scripts/run_browser_node.py's module
    // docstring for the full investigation.
    persona3 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        class="persona browser",
        label="Persona Session 3 (single-shot subprocess)",
        timeout="3600",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/run_browser_node.py\" --role persona --bundle \"${browser_bundle}\" --target-url \"${target_url}\" --api-key \"${api_key}\" --output-dir \"${output_dir}\" --report-path \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona3}.md\" --persona-name \"${persona3}\" --personas-dir \"${personas_dir}\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --timeout-s 3500 || true"
    ]

    check_persona3 -> persona3 [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_persona3 -> check_synthesis [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    persona3 -> verify_persona3

    verify_persona3 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Persona 3 artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" persona \"${output_dir}/persona-${persona3}.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_persona3 -> check_synthesis  [label="ok",       condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_persona3 -> persona3         [label="retry",    condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_persona3 -> hard_stop_persona3 [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    hard_stop_persona3 [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: persona-3 artifact missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.persona3-retry-count\"; printf 'FATAL: %s/persona-%s.md never satisfied its artifact contract after persona3 ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). This is the sequential persona chain -- synthesis needs every persona report. Inspect node status.json logs, .session-manifest-persona-%s.json for browser-ran proof counts, to see why persona3 reported success without writing a contract-satisfying file (e.g. max_agent_turns exhaustion mid-session, or zero real browser navigations detected). The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" \"${persona3}\" \"${persona3}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 5 — SYNTHESIS
    // ==================================================================
    // check_synthesis only guards the MAIN-LINE entry into synthesis (the
    // first pass after crash-resume). The human-requested revision loop
    // (gate_feedback -> synthesis, below) bypasses this guard entirely and
    // always re-runs synthesis — that is deliberate; a revision must
    // overwrite research-spec.md, not skip because it already exists.
    //
    // RESUME-SAFE HASH CLEANUP (4th council round): the "todo" branch
    // below now also clears any leftover .pre-revision-spec-hash BEFORE
    // proceeding to a fresh main-line synthesis run. Reasoning: that hash
    // file is a snapshot placed ONLY by snapshot_spec_hash, immediately
    // before a genuine human-requested revision's synthesis call, and is
    // meant to be consumed by check_revision_progress right after THAT
    // SAME synthesis call finishes. But if a prior run crashed between
    // snapshot_spec_hash writing it and check_revision_progress consuming
    // it (e.g. killed mid-revision), the file survives on disk. A fresh
    // resumed run reaching check_synthesis's "todo" edge (a genuine
    // main-line first pass in THIS run, no human feedback involved) would
    // otherwise carry that orphaned snapshot forward into ITS
    // verify_synthesis -> check_revision_progress hop, which has no way
    // to tell "stale leftover from an abandoned revision in a DIFFERENT
    // run" apart from "a real snapshot from a revision in progress THIS
    // run" -- both just mean "the file exists." Best case that ambiguity
    // is harmless (the freshly synthesized content merely happens to
    // differ from the orphaned hash); worst case a coincidental hash
    // match hard-stops a legitimate first pass with a nonsensical
    // "revision produced no change" fatal, for a revision that was never
    // requested in this run. Clearing the file here removes the
    // ambiguity at its source: only snapshot_spec_hash (reachable solely
    // via gate_feedback, i.e. an actual human revision request in the
    // CURRENT run's control flow) may cause the file to exist when
    // check_revision_progress runs.

    check_synthesis [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Spec exists?",
        tool_command="python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" spec \"${output_dir}/research-spec.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --findings-json \"${output_dir}/findings.json\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf done || { rm -f \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\"; printf todo; }"
    ]

    synthesis [
        shape=box,
        class="synthesis",
        label="Synthesis",
        fidelity="truncate",
        max_agent_turns="20",
        timeout="900",
        goal_gate=true,
        retry_target="synthesis",
        max_retries="2",
        prompt="You are conducting the SYNTHESIS stage of a simulated-user-research round — the final consolidation before human review. This round's Run ID is: $run_id (stamp this exact value, verbatim, wherever this prompt asks for a Run ID — never invent your own, never write 'unknown').

IMPORTANT — how your session ends: replying with text and no tool call ENDS your session immediately. Never narrate an intention ('let me write the spec now') as a text-only reply — call the tool in that same turn instead. Until BOTH research-spec.md and findings.json are written and verified, every reply must contain a tool call.

EPISTEMIC HONESTY (this is the whole point of this stage — read carefully): this round mixes [OBSERVED] machine-checkable facts (capture notes, bug tables with repro steps) with [SIMULATED] persona judgment (one LLM in three costumes, not three real humans) and your own [INFERRED] synthesis hypotheses. Never present these at equal authority. Use this confirmation vocabulary EXACTLY, never substitute a synonym:
  - REPRODUCED (N sessions) — an [OBSERVED] bug independently reproduced by N different sessions (personas and/or reviews) with matching repro steps.
  - OBSERVED (1 session) — an [OBSERVED] bug seen in exactly one session, with repro steps.
  - RAISED BY N/3 PERSONAS — a [SIMULATED] friction point or opinion multiple personas independently raised, with NO independent machine-checkable repro (a feeling, not a bug).
  - RELATED FINDING — two findings that plausibly share a root cause but were not independently confirmed the same way; RELATED is NEVER a synonym for CONFIRMED — do not upgrade a RELATED FINDING to REPRODUCED just because it feels connected.
Persona P-labels (P1/P2/P3 severity guesses in source reports) are SOURCE ATTRIBUTION ONLY, never your final severity call — you assign ONE severity taxonomy yourself: CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW, justified by the product's own stated promise or observed behavior, never by a persona's personal preference or backstory. If you demote a persona's P1 to something lower, say so explicitly: 'Demoted from P1 because: <reason>.' Parked Items can never sit above MEDIUM — if something is genuinely CRITICAL/HIGH it does not belong in Parked Items, full stop.

Read EVERY artifact in $output_dir that EXISTS (check each path before reading it — do not assume all six are present):
  - $output_dir/capture-notes.md
  - $output_dir/review-ia.md
  - $output_dir/review-responsive.md
  - $output_dir/persona-$persona1.md
  - $output_dir/persona-$persona2.md
  - $output_dir/persona-$persona3.md
  - (if present from a prior revision pass) $output_dir/research-spec.md — if this file already exists, treat your prior version as a DRAFT to revise, not a blank slate. If a human provided feedback in this turn (it will appear above this prompt as 'Human response at gate ...'), that feedback is the primary driver of what to change — read it carefully and apply it. A revision pass that does not actually change research-spec.md is treated as a hard failure downstream (see the .dot's revision-staleness check) — make a real, substantive change in response to the feedback, not a cosmetic no-op.

DEFENSIVE INPUT CHECK — do this first, before synthesizing: the upstream pipeline enforces file-existence guards on capture and each persona (they hard-stop the whole run if missing), but the two design-review artifacts (review-ia.md, review-responsive.md) run in a parallel branch with error_policy=continue, so it is possible to reach this stage with one or both of them absent. Never silently proceed as if a missing artifact simply doesn't exist / was never in scope. Track every missing path for the Provenance appendix (see Output below) — do not fabricate or infer its content, and do not quietly omit any cross-reference claims that would have depended on it.

Task:
Consolidate all findings into a SINGLE implementation-ready spec, built ONLY from the artifacts that actually exist. Cross-reference across artifacts using the confirmation vocabulary above — adjust denominators honestly if a source is missing. Do not simply concatenate the source documents — synthesize, deduplicate, and prioritize. For every finding tagged evidence_tier OBSERVED, carry its FULL repro steps INTO the spec (not just a pointer back to the source report — a human reading only the spec must be able to replay it). If a source report tagged something `PROBE (scripted in brief):`, preserve that tag in the spec — a scripted probe finding is still legitimate, but it must never be presented as a spontaneous discovery. If a source report marked a claim `HYPOTHESIS:` (a guess about real, non-simulated users), carry that marking into the spec unchanged — never launder a HYPOTHESIS into a confirmed finding.

Output (TWO files, both required — the spec is for humans, findings.json is for machines):

FILE 1 — Write (or overwrite, on a revision pass) $output_dir/research-spec.md with, in this exact order:
  > Simulated session: an LLM synthesizing across simulated persona sessions and design reviews of a real instance. Product behaviors (bug tables, timings, network checks) cited below were observed in-session and are reproducible. Persona verdicts and reactions cited below are simulation — hypotheses about this user class, not testimony.

  Run ID: $run_id

  ## Verdict Summary
  (markdown table: | Persona | Verdict (simulated) | Dealbreaker one-liner | — one row per persona, pulled from each persona report's Verdict section)
  ## Findings At a Glance
  (markdown table: | # | Sev | Title | Evidence tier | Confirmation | — one row per finding, # matching findings.json's id field, Sev one of CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW, Evidence tier one of OBSERVED/SIMULATED/INFERRED, Confirmation using the exact vocabulary above)
  ## Summary
  (≤3 sentences: overall health, biggest risk, biggest opportunity — this is the 60-second read, keep it tight)
  ## Detailed Findings
  (one subsection per finding from Findings At a Glance, in the same order and numbering; each: full description, evidence tier + confirmation restated, REPRO STEPS in full for every OBSERVED finding, suggested fix, and 'Demoted from P1 because: ...' if applicable)
  ## Prioritized Changes
  (ranked list, most important first; each with rationale tied back to a specific finding #)
  ## Copy Fixes
  (specific text changes: current wording -> suggested wording, with the reason)
  ## Parked Items
  (real issues that are lower priority or out of scope for this round — do not lose them, just don't block on them; NEVER above MEDIUM severity — if something belongs here it is MEDIUM or LOW by definition)
  ## Sources
  (list every artifact you actually read, so a human can trace any claim back to its evidence)
  ## Provenance
  (machine-status plumbing lives here, not in the 60-second-read sections above: '### Missing inputs' — list any of the six expected artifact paths that were absent when this synthesis ran, and what that means for the confidence of any related findings above; write 'None — all expected inputs were present.' if nothing was missing. '### Source paths' — the six expected artifact paths and whether each was present.)

The spec must be substantive and complete before you finish — a bare section skeleton does not satisfy this task. Never write a placeholder like \"(in progress)\" or \"to be completed\" anywhere in the file.

FILE 2 — Write (or overwrite, on a revision pass) $output_dir/findings.json as a SINGLE JSON object (not wrapped in markdown fences, valid JSON, parseable by `python3 -m json.tool`):
  {
    \"run_id\": \"$run_id\",
    \"findings\": [
      {
        \"id\": \"F-01\",
        \"title\": \"short finding title\",
        \"severity\": \"CRITICAL\" | \"HIGH\" | \"MEDIUM\" | \"LOW\",
        \"evidence_tier\": \"OBSERVED\" | \"SIMULATED\" | \"INFERRED\",
        \"confirmation\": \"REPRODUCED\" | \"OBSERVED_SINGLE\" | \"RAISED_BY_PERSONAS\" | \"RELATED\",
        \"repro\": [\"step 1\", \"step 2\"],
        \"sources\": [\"persona-marisol.md\", \"review-ia.md\"]
      }
    ]
  }
Every finding in Findings At a Glance / Detailed Findings in the spec must have a matching entry here with the SAME id and SAME severity/evidence_tier/confirmation values — the spec and findings.json are two views of the same data, never allowed to disagree. `repro` may be an empty list for SIMULATED/INFERRED findings (they have no machine repro by definition) but MUST be non-empty for every OBSERVED finding. `sources` must always be non-empty.

Before reporting anything, verify your own work with tool calls: run `wc -c $output_dir/research-spec.md` and confirm it is at least 1500 bytes; run `python3 -m json.tool $output_dir/findings.json` and confirm it parses with no error; confirm research-spec.md's second non-blank line reads exactly `Run ID: $run_id`. Only after seeing ALL THREE confirmed should you report success (via report_outcome if available, else a brief final text summary)."
    ]

    check_synthesis -> synthesis [label="todo", condition="context.tool.last_line=todo"]
    check_synthesis -> gate [label="skip", condition="context.tool.last_line=done"]
    synthesis -> verify_synthesis

    // DEFECT 2 fix, synthesis: requirement (b) is EVERY artifact-producing
    // box node, and synthesis is one, despite already carrying
    // goal_gate=true + retry_target="synthesis". That existing mechanism
    // (spec 3.4) only re-checks the node's REPORTED outcome.status at
    // pipeline-exit time -- it is the same class of gap as capture/persona
    // (a node can report success without having written the file). This
    // file-ground-truth check is additive defense-in-depth ahead of the
    // human gate: same ok/retry/hard_stop shape as the other stages, and
    // its "retry" edge reuses the SAME self-retry target the goal_gate
    // already establishes (synthesis), so the two mechanisms cooperate
    // rather than fight.
    verify_synthesis [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Synthesis artifact verified?",
        tool_command="if python3 \"${sur_repo_dir}/scripts/validate_artifact.py\" spec \"${output_dir}/research-spec.md\" --run-id \"${run_id}\" --findings-json \"${output_dir}/findings.json\" --require-exact; then rm -f \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count\"; printf ok; else cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 2 ]; then printf hard_stop; else printf retry; fi; else printf hard_stop; fi; fi"
    ]

    verify_synthesis -> check_revision_progress [label="ok",   condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]
    verify_synthesis -> synthesis          [label="retry",     condition="context.tool.last_line=retry"]
    verify_synthesis -> hard_stop_synthesis [label="hard stop", condition="context.tool.last_line=hard_stop"]

    hard_stop_synthesis [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: research-spec.md missing",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.synthesis-retry-count\" \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\"; printf 'FATAL: %s/research-spec.md never satisfied its artifact contract after synthesis ran 3 times (1 initial + 2 retries). There is nothing to show the human at the approval gate. Inspect node status.json logs to see why synthesis reported success without writing a contract-satisfying file (research-spec.md AND findings.json are both required). The retry counter has been cleared so a later re-run gets a fresh budget.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // REVISION STALENESS CHECK (hardening sprint): a failed or no-op
    // revision pass used to silently return the stale spec -- synthesis
    // is a box node (narrate-and-die is structurally possible there, see
    // run_browser_node.py's module docstring for why synthesis was
    // deliberately NOT converted to the single-shot wrapper), so this
    // check targets the EXACT failure mode named without giving up
    // goal_gate/reasoning_effort/human.gate.text injection. Mechanism:
    // gate_feedback's revision path snapshots research-spec.md's hash
    // via snapshot_spec_hash BEFORE re-running synthesis; this node
    // compares the POST-synthesis hash against that snapshot. If the
    // snapshot file does not exist at all (the normal first-pass path,
    // which never goes through gate_feedback), this node passes through
    // unconditionally -- there is nothing to compare a first pass
    // against. The snapshot file is consumed (deleted) on every run
    // through this node, revision or not, so a later independent
    // revision starts with a clean slate.
    //
    // 4th HARDENING CYCLE RE-AFFIRMATION: review_ia/review_responsive
    // were converted to single-shot tool nodes this cycle after
    // review_responsive hit the narrate-and-die shape 3x in round 4 (see
    // this graph's header comment and their own node comments below).
    // synthesis was re-evaluated against the SAME evidence and
    // deliberately left as-is: it has zero narrate-and-die incidents in
    // this pipeline's history (unlike review_responsive, which now has
    // one), and its ONE structurally-possible variant of the failure
    // mode -- a revision pass that narrates instead of rewriting
    // research-spec.md -- is exactly what THIS node (check_revision_progress)
    // catches via the hash-staleness check, not left to hope. Converting
    // synthesis would forfeit goal_gate/retry_target (spec 3.4),
    // reasoning_effort=high, and the engine's automatic human.gate.text
    // injection (backend.py step 5) for a benefit synthesis does not
    // currently need. If synthesis is EVER observed dying the loop-agent
    // text-only-reply shape directly (not just via a stale-but-still-written
    // revision), that observation is new evidence and this decision should
    // be revisited the same way the review nodes' was -- see
    // run_browser_node.py's module docstring, "--role synthesis WAS
    // CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT", for the mechanics that would
    // need reproducing.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    check_revision_progress [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Revision actually changed the spec?",
        tool_command="if [ ! -f \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\" ]; then printf changed; else old_hash=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\" 2>/dev/null || printf ''); new_hash=$(sha256sum \"${output_dir}/research-spec.md\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1); rm -f \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\"; if [ \"$old_hash\" = \"$new_hash\" ] && [ -n \"$new_hash\" ]; then printf stale; else printf changed; fi; fi"
    ]

    check_revision_progress -> gate                     [label="changed", condition="context.tool.last_line=changed"]
    check_revision_progress -> hard_stop_revision_stale  [label="stale",   condition="context.tool.last_line=stale"]

    hard_stop_revision_stale [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="HALT: revision produced no change",
        tool_command="printf 'FATAL: %s/research-spec.md is byte-identical to its pre-revision snapshot -- synthesis ran (goal_gate+artifact-contract both reported success) but produced NO substantive change in response to the human'\''s feedback at the gate. This is the narrate-and-die class named in the hardening brief: a failed/no-op revision must never silently return the stale spec to the human as if the feedback were applied. Inspect the synthesis node'\''s prompt.md/response.md in the run'\''s logs to see what feedback was given and why it produced no textual change.\\n' \"${output_dir}\" 1>&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // ==================================================================
    // STAGE 6 — HUMAN GATE (approve / revise, capped / end)
    // ==================================================================

    gate [
        shape=hexagon,
        label="Approve, Revise, or End?",
        description="Research round complete. Review the consolidated spec below and choose how to proceed.",
        attachments_inline="$output_dir/research-spec.md"
    ]

    gate -> exit [label="[A] Approve the spec"]
    gate -> check_revision_cap [label="[R] Request a revision pass"]
    gate -> exit [label="[E] End without approving"]

    // File-based counter — deterministic and inspectable, not LLM-counted.
    // Caps the synthesis<->gate loop at 3 revisions so it cannot run away.
    check_revision_cap [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Revision cap check (max 3)",
        tool_command="cnt=$(cat \"${output_dir}/.revision-count\" 2>/dev/null || printf 0); cnt=$((cnt + 1)); if printf '%s' \"$cnt\" > \"${output_dir}/.revision-count.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.revision-count.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.revision-count\"; then if [ \"$cnt\" -gt 3 ]; then printf cap_reached; else printf ok; fi; else printf cap_reached; fi"
    ]

    check_revision_cap -> revision_cap_reached [label="cap reached", condition="context.tool.last_line=cap_reached"]
    check_revision_cap -> gate_feedback [label="ok", condition="context.tool.last_line=ok"]

    revision_cap_reached [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Revision Cap Reached",
        tool_command="rm -f \"${output_dir}/.revision-count\"; printf '\\n\\n---\\n**Note:** revision cap (3) reached; spec finalized as-is at this round.\\n' >> \"${output_dir}/research-spec.md\" && printf done"
    ]
    revision_cap_reached -> exit

    gate_feedback [
        shape=hexagon,
        mode="freeform",
        label="What should change in the spec?",
        description="Describe what needs to change. Your feedback is passed directly to the synthesis re-run."
    ]

    // REVISION STALENESS (hardening sprint): snapshot research-spec.md's
    // hash BEFORE synthesis re-runs, so check_revision_progress (wired
    // after verify_synthesis, upstream of `gate`) can hard-stop loudly if
    // synthesis produced a byte-identical spec -- see that node's comment
    // for the full mechanism. A missing/unreadable current spec (should
    // never happen here since check_revision_cap only reaches this point
    // after a spec already passed its gate once) degrades to an empty
    // hash rather than failing this node -- check_revision_progress
    // treats an empty stored hash as "no prior snapshot" and passes
    // through, which is the safe default (never block a revision on this
    // snapshot step itself failing).
    snapshot_spec_hash [
        shape=parallelogram,
        label="Snapshot spec hash (pre-revision)",
        tool_command="sha256sum \"${output_dir}/research-spec.md\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d' ' -f1 > \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash.tmp\" && mv \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash.tmp\" \"${output_dir}/.pre-revision-spec-hash\"; printf done"
    ]

    gate_feedback -> snapshot_spec_hash

    // No guard here by design — a revision must always re-run synthesis.
    // The human's freeform text is auto-injected as a "Human response at
    // gate ..." preface on synthesis's next prompt by the engine (see
    // gotchas: consume-once human.gate.text injection).
    snapshot_spec_hash -> synthesis
}
